270: 
271: You can optionally load configuration from a different module using the `externalModuleName` option. This should only be used when absolutely necessary, as it can create coupling between modules:
272: 
273: ```ts
274: const otherModuleConfig = useConfig({ externalModuleName: "@openmrs/esm-other-module" });
275: ```
276: 
277: **Registering config schemas:**
278: 
279: In Core v5+ modules, config schemas should be registered in the `startupApp()` function:
280: 
281: ```ts
282: import { defineConfigSchema } from "@openmrs/esm-framework";
283: import { configSchema } from "./config-schema";
284: 
285: const moduleName = "@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor";
286: 
287: export function startupApp() {
288:   defineConfigSchema(moduleName, configSchema);
289: }
290: ```
291: 
292: This ensures the schema is registered before the module's components are rendered.
293: 
294: **Automatic schema features:**
295: 
296: Every module automatically receives an implicit schema that includes two reserved configuration keys:
297: 
298: - **`Display conditions`** - Used by the extension system to control extension visibility (only relevant for extensions)
299: - **`Translation overrides`** - Used for per-language translation overrides. See [Configure translations](/docs/configure-o3/configure-translations) for details on how implementers use this feature.
300: 
301: This implicit schema is available immediately, even before you call `defineConfigSchema()`. This allows translation overrides to be loaded early, which is important because the translation system requires them before modules can fully initialize. When you define your custom schema, it is automatically merged with the implicit schema, so you don't need to (and should not) define these reserved keys yourself.
302: 
303: #### Support in other frameworks (Angular, Vue, Svelte, etc.)
304: 
305: This hasn't been implemented yet, but you are welcome to implement it! See the [Contributing guide](/docs/frontend-modules/contributing).
306: 
307: #### Typing
308: 
309: It is nice to be able to have type validation for your use of configuration. To accomplish this, define an interface alongside your config schema.
310: 
311: ```ts
312: import { defineConfigSchema, Type } from "@openmrs/esm-framework";
313: 
314: defineConfigSchema("@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor", {
315:   hologram: {
316:     color: {
317:       _type: Type.Boolean,
318:       _default: false,
319:       _description: "Whether the hologram supports color display.",
320:     },
321:   },
322: });
323: 
324: export interface HologramDoctorConfig {
325:   hologram: {
326:     color: boolean;
327:   };
328: }
329: ```
330: 
331: You can then use this typing information when calling the config functions:
332: 
333: ```ts
334: const config = useConfig<HologramDoctorConfig>();
335: ```
336: 
337: #### Extension-specific configuration
338: 
339: Extensions can have their own configuration schemas that are separate from their parent module's configuration. This is useful when an extension needs configuration options that don't apply to the module as a whole.
340: 
341: To define an extension-specific config schema, use `defineExtensionConfigSchema`:
342: 
343: ```ts
344: import { defineExtensionConfigSchema, Type } from "@openmrs/esm-framework";
345: 
346: export function startupApp() {
347:   defineExtensionConfigSchema("my-extension-name", {
348:     customSetting: {
349:       _type: Type.String,
350:       _default: "default value",
351:       _description: "A setting specific to this extension"
352:     }
353:   });
354: }
355: ```
356: 
357: The extension name should match the `name` property of the extension as defined in `routes.json`. When an extension has its own config schema, base values for that schema are provided under the extension name, not under the module name. `useConfig()` within that extension still returns the module's normal configuration plus the extension's configuration. Extension-specific values take precedence when the same top-level key exists in both places.
